Relevant tables & columns for the task  
• interest_metrics.month_year : month‐stamp we must display (format already “MM‑YYYY”)  
• interest_metrics.interest_id : foreign‑key that tells us which interest the metric belongs to  
• interest_metrics.composition : metric we are ranking on (we need each interest’s highest value)  
• interest_map.id : primary‑key of the interest category (join to interest_metrics.interest_id)  
• interest_map.interest_name : descriptive category name we must show  
  
Possible filter/logic conditions  
• Join condition ⇒ interest_metrics.interest_id = interest_map.id  
• To find each category’s “best” month ⇒ use MAX(composition) per interest_id  
• From those maxima choose 10 largest (top) and 10 smallest (bottom)  

Below are ten exploratory → solution‑building queries (all SELECT‑only, each limited to 20 rows).  

```sql
--Description: 1) All distinct calendar months present in the metrics table
SELECT DISTINCT "month_year"
FROM "interest_metrics"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) Get the first 20 distinct interest_id values that have metrics
SELECT DISTINCT "interest_id"
FROM "interest_metrics"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) Inspect the master list of interest names
SELECT "id", "interest_name"
FROM "interest_map"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) Quick sanity‑check: join a handful of rows to see id → name mapping
SELECT im."month_year",
       im."interest_id",
       mp."interest_name",
       im."composition"
FROM   "interest_metrics" AS im
JOIN   "interest_map"     AS mp
       ON im."interest_id" = mp."id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) Distribution sample – same join but ordered by highest composition
SELECT im."month_year",
       mp."interest_name",
       im."composition"
FROM   "interest_metrics" AS im
JOIN   "interest_map"     AS mp
       ON im."interest_id" = mp."id"
ORDER  BY im."composition" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Compute each category’s single highest composition across all months
SELECT im."interest_id",
       MAX(im."composition") AS "max_composition"
FROM   "interest_metrics" AS im
GROUP  BY im."interest_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Retrieve the TOP 10 categories (by that max composition) with their best month
WITH best_month AS (
    SELECT im."interest_id",
           im."month_year",
           im."composition",
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY im."interest_id"
                              ORDER BY im."composition" DESC) AS rn
    FROM   "interest_metrics" AS im
)
SELECT bm."month_year",
       mp."interest_name",
       bm."composition"
FROM   best_month AS bm
JOIN   "interest_map" AS mp
       ON bm."interest_id" = mp."id"
WHERE  bm.rn = 1                      -- keep only the best month per interest
ORDER  BY bm."composition" DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) Retrieve the BOTTOM 10 categories (lowest max compositions)
WITH best_month AS (
    SELECT im."interest_id",
           im."month_year",
           im."composition",
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY im."interest_id"
                              ORDER BY im."composition" DESC) AS rn
    FROM   "interest_metrics" AS im
)
SELECT bm."month_year",
       mp."interest_name",
       bm."composition"
FROM   best_month AS bm
JOIN   "interest_map" AS mp
       ON bm."interest_id" = mp."id"
WHERE  bm.rn = 1
ORDER  BY bm."composition" ASC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
--Description: 9) Union the TOP & BOTTOM lists together for a combined 20‑row set
WITH best_month AS (
    SELECT im."interest_id",
           im."month_year",
           im."composition",
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY im."interest_id"
                              ORDER BY im."composition" DESC) AS rn
    FROM   "interest_metrics" AS im
), extremes AS (
    SELECT bm."month_year",
           mp."interest_name",
           bm."composition"
    FROM   best_month AS bm
    JOIN   "interest_map" AS mp
           ON bm."interest_id" = mp."id"
    WHERE  bm.rn = 1
)
SELECT * FROM extremes
ORDER BY "composition" DESC
LIMIT 10

UNION ALL

SELECT * FROM extremes
ORDER BY "composition" ASC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
--Description: 10) Final formatted answer (combined top & bottom, clearly labelled)
WITH best_month AS (
    SELECT im."interest_id",
           im."month_year",
           im."composition",
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY im."interest_id"
                              ORDER BY im."composition" DESC) AS rn
    FROM   "interest_metrics" AS im
),
extremes AS (
    SELECT bm."month_year",
           mp."interest_name",
           bm."composition",
           RANK() OVER (ORDER BY bm."composition" DESC) AS desc_rank,
           RANK() OVER (ORDER BY bm."composition" ASC)  AS asc_rank
    FROM   best_month AS bm
    JOIN   "interest_map" AS mp
           ON bm."interest_id" = mp."id"
    WHERE  bm.rn = 1
),
top_10    AS (SELECT * FROM extremes ORDER BY "composition" DESC LIMIT 10),
bottom_10 AS (SELECT * FROM extremes ORDER BY "composition" ASC  LIMIT 10)

SELECT 'TOP'    AS "segment", "month_year", "interest_name", "composition" FROM top_10
UNION ALL
SELECT 'BOTTOM' AS "segment", "month_year", "interest_name", "composition" FROM bottom_10
ORDER BY "segment" DESC, "composition" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries progressively explore the necessary columns, map IDs to names, and culminate in the final result ‑‑ the 10 highest‑performing and 10 lowest‑performing interest categories based on their best (maximum) composition over all months, along with the relevant month (MM‑YYYY) and composition value.
